{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260923-001-r11s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260923-001-r11s0\ntitle: \"Select narrow load lanes without a word shifter\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The MEM load path shifts the 32-bit RAM output by a variable byte offset,\n  although byte and halfword loads consume only one narrow lane. That selection\n  sits between the data memory read and MEM/WB registers on every narrow load.\n  Earlier live-load forwarding reduced cycles but lengthened this path enough\n  to lose fitness; a local extraction change can shorten it without changing\n  pipeline timing or adding a bypass.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the variable 32-bit load shift with a four-way 8-bit byte-lane\n  selection, a two-way 16-bit halfword selection using address bit 1, and a\n  direct word path. Sign- or zero-extend only the selected narrow value. A\n  halfword access with address bit 0 set already traps and suppresses register\n  writeback, so it does not require an additional shifted-data case. Preserve\n  the existing alignment traps, byte masks, raw RVFI memory word, bus holds,\n  and single retirement. This should reduce the RAM-to-MEM/WB LUT depth and\n  improve Fmax while leaving CoreMark cycles unchanged.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Replace the full-word variable shift with direct byte and aligned-halfword lane muxes, then extend the selected value; retain masks, traps, and RVFI raw-memory fields.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Exercise signed and unsigned byte and halfword loads at every legal offset, word loads, misaligned halfword traps, and stalled load retirement with exact RVFI values.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
