{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_17", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"rg -n 'shift|shifter|barrel|right shift|left shift' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260923-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Move high-half multiply off the single-cycle EX path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The divider change raised fitness by removing rarely used wide arithmetic\\nfrom the one-cycle EX path. The ALU still builds three full 64-bit products\\nfor MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU. Those high-half product paths must meet\\nthe clock period even when CoreMark is executing ordinary 32-bit operations.\\nCoreMark's matrix arithmetic needs the low-half MUL throughput, so that\\noperation should remain single-cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The divider change raised fitness by removing rarely used wide arithmetic\\nfrom the one-cycle EX path. The ALU still builds three full 64-bit products\\nfor MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU. Those high-half product paths must meet\\nthe clock period even when CoreMark is executing ordinary 32-bit operations.\\nCoreMark's matrix arithmetic needs the low-half MUL throughput, so that\\noperation should remain single-cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep MUL as a single-cycle 32-bit low-product operation. Move MULH, MULHU,\\nand MULHSU into one shared iterative high-half multiplier attached to EX,\\nremoving their wide combinational products from the ALU. Form the unsigned\\n64-bit product with a 32-step shift-add engine using a roughly 33-bit adder;\\ncorrect its high word for signed operands in separate completion cycles.\\nCapture forwarded operands and RVFI metadata once, hold younger instructions\\nwhile a high-half multiply runs, let older instructions drain, and present\\none completed EX/MEM result only when the boundary accepts it. Keep the\\nresult stable through dmem backpressure. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, use the\\nexact prescribed stand-in result with a short but real start/complete\\nhandshake so the existing liveness depth remains meaningful. This should\\nshorten the EX critical path while preserving low-half MUL throughput and\\nordered single-channel retirement.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [\n…[13251 of 16323 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8d6a4e3ba082618b]…\n with direct byte and aligned-halfword lane muxes, then extend the selected value; retain masks, traps, and RVFI raw-memory fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Exercise signed and unsigned byte and halfword loads at every legal offset, word loads, misaligned halfword traps, and stalled load retirement with exact RVFI values.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 396.83, \"delta_pct\": -2.87, \"vs_baseline\": 40.31, \"fmax_mhz\": 178.25, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5646, \"ff\": 2212, \"seeds\": [147.45, 178.25, 184.03], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-23T19:11:18.449498Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-001-r11s0 (regression, -2.87%): The MEM load shift feeds only the low 16 bits, allowing synthesis to prune unused shifter outputs; explicit byte and halfword lane muxes add no CoreMark cycle benefit and can map to a slower RAM-to-MEM/WB path.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
