{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260923-001-r3s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260923-001-r3s0\ntitle: \"Share the divider comparison and subtraction\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The iterative divider currently compares a 33-bit trial remainder against\n  the divisor and separately subtracts the same operands every RUN cycle.\n  These overlapping carry networks consume LUTs and routing even though\n  CoreMark rarely divides. The prior RVFI counter segmentation improved\n  fitness by shortening a timing path without changing the cycle count;\n  this is another small timing and placement opportunity in the current core.\nhypothesis: |\n  Compute one 33-bit subtraction of the low 32 bits of the trial remainder\n  and the divisor. Use its borrow bit, together with trial[32], to decide\n  whether subtraction succeeds; use the low 32 result bits as the candidate\n  remainder. This replaces the separate wide comparison and subtraction\n  while preserving all 32 restoring steps, the existing EX interlock, and\n  exact DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU results. Keep divide-by-zero, signed correction,\n  completion holding, and the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch unchanged. Reduced\n  duplicated arithmetic and routing should improve FPGA Fmax with identical\n  CoreMark cycles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n    description: \"Derive the quotient decision and next remainder from one widened subtract, accounting for trial[32] when the shifted remainder exceeds 32 bits.\"\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: \"Add directed high-bit trial, divide-by-zero, and signed-edge vectors to check the shared subtract across all four divide and remainder operations.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
