{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260923-001-r5s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260923-001-r5s0\ntitle: \"Bank the register-file read muxes\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  After the one-hot ALU-select improvement, a routed seed's critical path\n  runs from the instruction input through a register-file source read into\n  the ID/EX operand register (5.66 ns). The present indexed reads infer wide\n  32-to-1 muxes, followed by write-through bypass muxes. CoreMark performs\n  two such reads for most instructions, so shortening this path can raise\n  Fmax without changing its cycle count or the bus interfaces.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the same 32 x 32 flip-flop register array and synchronous write port,\n  but implement each asynchronous read as four local eight-register banks.\n  Decode the low three address bits once per read port into one-hot selects,\n  OR the selected register within each bank, then select one of the four bank\n  results with the high two address bits. Keep x0 hardwired to zero and retain\n  the existing same-cycle WB write-through priority. This bounded local read\n  network should map to fewer serial LUT and routing levels on the measured\n  instruction-to-ID/EX operand path, improving median Fmax while preserving\n  the single-issue retirement stream and all RVFI values.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Replace both indexed 32-way reads with four eight-word one-hot local reads and a four-bank select; preserve reset, synchronous writes, x0, and same-cycle write-through semantics.\"\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: \"Exercise both read ports with randomized addresses and writes, including same-cycle collisions, x0, and bank boundaries, against a software register model.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
