{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"rg -n 'structural|hyp-20260923-002-r13s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260923-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple DIV and REM with an iterative EX unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench core puts four full-width combinational divide and remainder operations\\non its one-cycle EX datapath. That costs LUTs and can constrain Fmax for every\\nCoreMark instruction, while the timed matrix, list, and state loop has effectively\\nno divide traffic. A closely related reference core gained Fmax and shed thousands\\nof LUTs after moving these operations to a multicycle unit. The bench baseline has\\nonly 437 LUT4s of margin under its 10000-LUT4 target, making this structural change\\nespecially promising.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench core puts four full-width combinational divide and remainder operations\\non its one-cycle EX datapath. That costs LUTs and can constrain Fmax for every\\nCoreMark instruction, while the timed matrix, list, and state loop has effectively\\nno divide traffic. A closely related reference core gained Fmax and shed thousands\\nof LUTs after moving these operations to a multicycle unit. The bench baseline has\\nonly 437 LUT4s of margin under its 10000-LUT4 target, making this structural change\\nespecially promising.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU datapaths with one 32-step\\nrestoring divider attached to EX. On a divide-class instruction, capture the\\npost-forwarded operands and complete ID/EX metadata once, freeze younger fetch\\nand decode, and let older MEM/WB work drain through invalid EX/MEM bubbles.\\nWhen the divider finishes, advance exactly one EX/MEM result with the captured\\nmetadata; preserve dmem-stall priority and release the upstream interlock only\\nwhen that result is accepted. Keep all RV32I operations and four MUL variants\\nsingle-cycle. Implement exact RV32M signed, unsigned, divide-by-zero, and\\nINT_MIN/-1 behavior. Preserve the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS result substitutions and\\nexercise the same launch/complete interlock in formal mode. This should raise\\nmedian Fmax with negligible ch\n…[51277 of 54349 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:bfa122321889448f]…\nlue directly for the register-file write, with existing valid and reg-write gating.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Check adjacent ALU and load dependencies, write-first and MEM/WB forwarding, repeated dmem stalls, exact RVFI destination data, and one ordered retirement per instruction.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 393.4, \"delta_pct\": -3.71, \"vs_baseline\": 39.1, \"fmax_mhz\": 176.71, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5634, \"ff\": 2165, \"seeds\": [160.33, 179.89, 176.71], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-23T19:26:33.046431Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 12, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-002-r12s1 (regression, -3.71%): Registering the selected MEM/WB destination removes the WB forwarding mux but adds a 32-bit ALU/load select after load extraction on the RAM-to-MEM/WB path, which can cost more routed timing than the shorter WB path saves without reducing CoreMark cycles.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
