{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('id') in ('hyp-20260923-001-r5s0','hyp-20260923-001-r8s0'):\n  print(d['id'],d.get('title'));print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'] if isinstance(d['hypothesis'],dict) else d['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260923-001-r5s0 Bank the register-file read muxes\nKeep the same 32 x 32 flip-flop register array and synchronous write port,\nbut implement each asynchronous read as four local eight-register banks.\nDecode the low three address bits once per read port into one-hot selects,\nOR the selected register within each bank, then select one of the four bank\nresults with the high two address bits. Keep x0 hardwired to zero and retain\nthe existing same-cycle WB write-through priority. This bounded local read\nnetwork should map to fewer serial LUT and routing levels on the measured\ninstruction-to-ID/EX operand path, improving median Fmax while preserving\nthe single-issue retirement stream and all RVFI values.\n\nhyp-20260923-001-r8s0 Retiming the register-file write bypass into EX\nCapture the raw register-file read values in ID/EX. At the same edge, save\nthe WB write data once and latch one WB-address-match bit per source, gated\nby write enable and nonzero register number. In EX, choose the saved WB\nvalue when its match bit is set, below the existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB\nforwarding priorities. Hold this metadata with ID/EX during data-memory\nand divide stalls, and clear its effect on reset or an invalid bubble.\nPreserve the externally tested write-first register-file outputs while\nexposing raw reads for the core, so synthesis can prune the unused\nID-stage bypass muxes. This should shorten the critical read path while\npreserving the exact operands, RVFI values, bus behavior, and cycle count.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
