{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-001-r1s0 (regression, -8.32%): A combinational MEM-to-EX load-data bypass saved CoreMark load-use cycles, but its load-extraction and forwarding path lowered Fmax from 127.03 to 111.2 MHz, outweighing the cycle gain.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-002-r1s1 (improvement, +23.51%): CoreMark's rare divide traffic makes a shared 32-step EX divider worthwhile: moving four combinational DIV/REM operators off the one-cycle ALU path improves fitness despite stalling divide instructions.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-003-r1s2 (regression, +23.07%): A 16-entry tagged fetch BTB saved roughly 6% of CoreMark cycles, but its 10,412 LUT4 footprint exceeded the 10,000-LUT4 target and its 147.4 MHz Fmax left it just behind the iterative divider's fitness.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-001-r2s0 (improvement, +3.62%): Splitting the 64-bit RVFI retirement counter into 16-bit increments gated by lower-chunk wrap improves FPGA fitness by shortening the wide carry path while preserving strict +1 order.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-002-r2s1 (regression, -3.45%): CoreMark's timed path has no MULH-family instructions, so the fitness loss after adding a 32-step high-half EX unit and interlock points to worsened Fmax in the changed datapath rather than multiply stalls.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-001-r3s0 (regression, -5.68%): Sharing the divider's comparison and difference through a 33-bit subtraction makes the restoring decision depend on its borrow carry chain; with the same 32 steps, this arithmetic saving can worsen FPGA fitness.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-002-r3s1 (regression, -12.70%): A two-entry IF prefetch queue saved roughly 3% of CoreMark cycles, but its FIFO-head/live-bus bypass and queue control coincided with Fmax falling from 162.6 to 137.2 MHz, so fetch-side timing outweighed the hidden imem stalls.\n- 2026-0