{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260923-002-r1s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260923-002-r1s1\ntitle: \"Decouple DIV and REM with an iterative EX unit\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The bench core puts four full-width combinational divide and remainder operations\n  on its one-cycle EX datapath. That costs LUTs and can constrain Fmax for every\n  CoreMark instruction, while the timed matrix, list, and state loop has effectively\n  no divide traffic. A closely related reference core gained Fmax and shed thousands\n  of LUTs after moving these operations to a multicycle unit. The bench baseline has\n  only 437 LUT4s of margin under its 10000-LUT4 target, making this structural change\n  especially promising.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU datapaths with one 32-step\n  restoring divider attached to EX. On a divide-class instruction, capture the\n  post-forwarded operands and complete ID/EX metadata once, freeze younger fetch\n  and decode, and let older MEM/WB work drain through invalid EX/MEM bubbles.\n  When the divider finishes, advance exactly one EX/MEM result with the captured\n  metadata; preserve dmem-stall priority and release the upstream interlock only\n  when that result is accepted. Keep all RV32I operations and four MUL variants\n  single-cycle. Implement exact RV32M signed, unsigned, divide-by-zero, and\n  INT_MIN/-1 behavior. Preserve the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS result substitutions and\n  exercise the same launch/complete interlock in formal mode. This should raise\n  median Fmax with negligible change to CoreMark's marker-to-marker cycle count.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 15\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Remove real DIV/REM arithmetic from the synthesized one-cycle ALU mux while preserving the single-cycle RV32I and MUL paths and ALTOPS compatibility.\"\n  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n    description: \"Add one iterative signed/unsigned quotient/remainder engine with start, busy, done, and result signaling, including all RV32M corner cases.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Launch the divider once using forwarded operands, retain the instruction's RVFI metadata, emit bubbles while busy, and inject its completed result exactly once.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Interlock IF and ID during division while allowing older instructions to drain; preserve load-use, redirect, imem, and dmem-stall priority.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Connect the EX divide handshake to the hazard controls without changing the nret=1 top-level interface or retirement order.\"\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: \"Keep all non-divide ALU checks and move the existing DIV/REM vectors to the new divider test without reducing arithmetic coverage.\"\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: \"Check real divider latency, back-to-back launches, all signed/unsigned corner cases, and exact results for the migrated vectors.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Include the new RTL source and check divide interlocks, dependent forwarding, and single ordered retirement through bus stalls.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
