{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"rg -n 'mulh|mulhsu|mulhu|divu?|remu?' bench/programs cores/bench  --glob '\"'!log.jsonl'\"' --glob '\"'!*.sv'\"' --glob '\"'!test_*.py'\"' | head -80\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/core.yaml:11:  coremark_iter_s: 300\ncores/bench/core.yaml:12:  coremark_per_mhz: 2.2\ncores/bench/core.yaml:17:  coremark_iter_s: 2.0e-06\ncores/bench/core.yaml:18:  coremark_per_mhz: 0.0\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:2:- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-002-r1s1 (improvement, +23.51%): CoreMark's rare divide traffic makes a shared 32-step EX divider worthwhile: moving four combinational DIV/REM operators off the one-cycle ALU path improves fitness despite stalling divide instructions.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:3:- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-003-r1s2 (regression, +23.07%): A 16-entry tagged fetch BTB saved roughly 6% of CoreMark cycles, but its 10,412 LUT4 footprint exceeded the 10,000-LUT4 target and its 147.4 MHz Fmax left it just behind the iterative divider's fitness.\nbench/programs/coremark/barebones_porting.md:9:The bare-bones folder in the CoreMark repository provides the bare minimum to allow CoreMark to be ported to a processor system. As the code inside does not have any dependency on OS, it is the best starting point for porting CoreMark to a baremetal system where OS is not used, such as a microcontroller or an embedded processor.\nbench/programs/coremark/barebones_porting.md:18:The CoreMark execution needs to execute for at least 10 seconds. Therefore when selecting a timer peripheral for timing measurement, you need to ensure that the timer can measure the whole duration of the coremark execution. For example, let's say you are using a microcontroller and that has a 24-bit timer, and use the 24-bit timer as the timing reference. If the device is running at 100MHz and the timer is setup to run using the processor's clock, the longest time that the timer can count is 0.16777 second before it overflows or reaches zero. To measure the execution time, you could setup that timer to interrupt at a rate of 1KHz, and increment a counter variable inside the interrupt service routine. With this arrangement, there is some software overhead but the result should still be quite accurate. \nbench/programs/coremark/barebones_por\n…[11489 of 14561 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:445849eb14a0d57a]…\nle data space will be divided to 3 parts:\nbench/programs/Makefile:7:# - COREMARK_SIZE: TOTAL_DATA_SIZE in coremark.h (#ifndef-guarded). 2000 is\nbench/programs/Makefile:16:#     make bench/programs/coremark.elf COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O2\nbench/programs/Makefile:22:           -T bench/programs/link.ld -Ibench/programs/coremark \\\nbench/programs/Makefile:23:           -Ibench/programs/coremark/baremetal \\\nbench/programs/Makefile:26:SRCS := bench/programs/coremark/core_list_join.c \\\nbench/programs/Makefile:27:        bench/programs/coremark/core_main.c \\\nbench/programs/Makefile:28:        bench/programs/coremark/core_matrix.c \\\nbench/programs/Makefile:29:        bench/programs/coremark/core_state.c \\\nbench/programs/Makefile:30:        bench/programs/coremark/core_util.c \\\nbench/programs/Makefile:31:        bench/programs/coremark/baremetal/core_portme.c \\\nbench/programs/Makefile:34:bench/programs/coremark.elf: $(SRCS)\nbench/programs/Makefile:42:all: bench/programs/coremark.elf bench/programs/selftest.elf\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
