{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-001-r1s0 (regression, -8.32%): A combinational MEM-to-EX load-data bypass saved CoreMark load-use cycles, but its load-extraction and forwarding path lowered Fmax from 127.03 to 111.2 MHz, outweighing the cycle gain.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-002-r1s1 (improvement, +23.51%): CoreMark's rare divide traffic makes a shared 32-step EX divider worthwhile: moving four combinational DIV/REM operators off the one-cycle ALU path improves fitness despite stalling divide instructions.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-003-r1s2 (regression, +23.07%): A 16-entry tagged fetch BTB saved roughly 6% of CoreMark cycles, but its 10,412 LUT4 footprint exceeded the 10,000-LUT4 target and its 147.4 MHz Fmax left it just behind the iterative divider's fitness.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-001-r2s0 (improvement, +3.62%): Splitting the 64-bit RVFI retirement counter into 16-bit increments gated by lower-chunk wrap improves FPGA fitness by shortening the wide carry path while preserving strict +1 order.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-002-r2s1 (regression, -3.45%): CoreMark's timed path has no MULH-family instructions, so the fitness loss after adding a 32-step high-half EX unit and interlock points to worsened Fmax in the changed datapath rather than multiply stalls.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\"|structural|hyp-20260923-002-r3s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260923-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple DIV and REM with an iterative EX unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench core puts four full-width combinational divide and remainder operations\\non its one-cycle EX datapath. That costs LUTs and can constrain Fmax for every\\nCoreMark instruction, while the timed matrix, list, and state loop has effectively\\nno divide traffic. A closely related reference core gained Fmax and shed thousands\\nof LUTs after moving these operations to a multicycle unit. The bench baseline has\\nonly 437 LUT4s of margin under its 10000-LUT4 target, making this structural change\\nespecially promising.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench core puts four full-width combinational divide and remainder operations\\non its one-cycle EX datapath. That costs LUTs and can constrain Fmax for every\\nCoreMark instruction, while the timed matrix, list, and state loop has effectively\\nno divide traffic. A closely related reference core gained Fmax and shed thousands\\nof LUTs after moving these operations to a multicycle unit. The bench baseline has\\nonly 437 LUT4s of margin under its 10000-LUT4 target, making this structural change\\nespecially promising.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU datapaths with one 32-step\\nrestoring divider attached to EX. On a divide-class instruction, capture the\\npost-forwarded operands and complete ID/EX metadata once, freeze younger fetch\\nand decode, and let older MEM/WB work drain through invalid EX/MEM bubbles.\\nWhen the divider finishes, advance exactly one EX/MEM result with the captured\\nmetadata; preserve dmem-stall priority and release the upstream interlock only\\nwhen 