{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"rg -n 'store.slot|pending.store|buffer.*store' cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl | head -3\"", "aggregated_output": "9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Retiring Store Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry FIFO for normal RAM stores. When a legal aligned store to the\\nRAM address range reaches MEM and the external dmem port is not immediately\\navailable, retire the store into the FIFO with its address, replicated write\\ndata, byte mask, and RVFI metadata, then let younger non-memory instructions\\ncontinue through the pipeline while the FIFO drains oldest-first on the dmem\\nport. Loads, stores when the FIFO is full, and all MMIO/marker/UART stores\\nmust wait until older buffered stores ha\n…[21649 of 24721 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e6b6fac453da1c33]…\nent when the slot drains.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` could not run in-place in this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a read-only path outside the writable worktree, and the script failed while removing stale staged files. I copied the same formal tree, wrapper, checks config, and current `rtl/*.sv` into `/tmp/codex-formal-r8s1.FkHGdP` and ran the unchanged `formal/run_all.sh` there.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- The riscv-formal wrapper ties `io_dmemReady` high, so formal proves the unchanged direct path but does not exercise pending-slot drain behavior. The slot path is covered by lint and structural review here; downstream random-dStall cosim remains the main gate for that behavior.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` from writable `/tmp` mirror: pass, `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n- `pytest -q test/test_pipeline.py`: pass, `1 passed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T05:00:14.609332\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 8}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
