{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'] in {'hyp-20260923-001-r4s0','hyp-20260923-002-r3s1','hyp-20260923-003-r1s2'}:\n  print('\\\\n'+d['id']+' '+d['title'])\n  print('fitness',d['fitness'],'fmax',d['fmax_mhz'],'cycles',d['cycles'],'lut4',d['lut4'])\n  print('hypothesis:',d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:1200])\n  print('notes:',d.get('implementation_notes','')[:1200])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'] in {'hyp-20260923-001-r4s0','hyp-20260923-002-r3s1','hyp-20260923-003-r1s2'}:\n  print('\\\\n'+d['id']+' '+d['title'])\n  print('fitness',d['fitness'],'fmax',d['fmax_mhz'],'cycles',d['cycles'],'lut4',d['lut4'])\n  print('hypothesis:',d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:1200])\n  print('notes:',d.get('implementation_notes','')[:1200])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\nhyp-20260923-003-r1s2 Predict control flow with a small branch target buffer\nfitness 348.08 fmax 147.38 cycles 4234052 lut4 10412\nhypothesis: Add a 16-entry direct-mapped, fully tagged branch target buffer at fetch. Each entry holds a target and a two-bit saturating direction counter; unconditional JAL/JALR entries always predict taken. On a tag miss, fetch proceeds sequentially. Train only when a valid control-flow instruction advances through EX, using its resolved aligned target and outcome. Carry the exact predicted next PC with the instruction into EX, and redirect/flush younger work only when it differs from the resolved next PC. Suppress training and recovery while EX is held by a dmem stall, then perform them when EX advances. Preserve the existing sequential next PC and trap behavior for misaligned targets, and correct stale predictions even if the instruction at a trained PC changes. This should save cycles on recurring CoreMark branches and calls while keeping the added lookup small enough to limit Fmax loss.\n\nnotes: \n\nhyp-20260923-002-r3s1 Decouple fetch from issue with a two-entry prefetch queue\nfitness 315.99 fmax 137.19 cycles 4341607 lut4 5647\nhypothesis: Give IF a two-entry FIFO of {PC, instruction} pairs and a separate fetch PC.\nFetch into free FIFO space whenever io_imemReady is high, including cycles\nwhen ID is held by load-use, dmem, or division. Issue the FIFO head when\navailable; when empty, bypass a ready imem word directly into the existing\nID/EX register, preserving the present zero-wait latency. An empty FIFO and\nan unready bus produce the existing bubble. Allow simultaneous dequeue and\nenqueue, but do not enqueue a bypassed word that ID consumes in that cycle;\nadvance the fetch PC only for accepted words. On an EX redirect,\ndiscard all queued wrong-path words, suppress same-cycle wrong-path issue,\nand restart fetching at the resolved target with redirect priority over\nboth bus and pipeline stalls. Keep each instruction's PC paired with its\nword for RVFI, and retain the nret=1 retirement interface and all existing\ntrap and memory semantics. This should recover part of the imem-stall cost\nwith only a small fetch-side register and mux footprint, leaving the EX\narithmetic critical path untouched.\n\nnotes: \n\nhyp-20260923-001-r4s0 Register one-hot ALU selects before execute\nfitness 392.16 fmax 176.15 cycles 4491817 lut4 5644\nhypothesis: Decode the legal ALU operation to one-hot selects in ID and register those\nselects with the existing ID/EX payload. In EX, compute the RV32I arithmetic,\nlogical, shift, and multiply candidates in parallel and select them with\nthe registered one-hot bits, avoiding a five-bit operation decode after the\nID/EX register. Retain the encoded operation for the divider handshake and\npreserve both real RV32M results and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions.\nKeep the existing forwarding, stall, trap, retirement, and nret=1 behavior.\nRemoving EX decode depth should improve Fmax with the same timed cycle count.\n\nnotes: \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
