{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['category']=='structural':\n  print('\\\\n'+d['id']+' '+d['title'])\n  print('HYP: '+str(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis']).replace('\\\\n',' ')[:1200])\n  print('RESULT:',d['outcome'],d['delta_pct'],d['fmax_mhz'],d['cycles'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nhyp-20260923-002-r1s1 Decouple DIV and REM with an iterative EX unit\nHYP: Replace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU datapaths with one 32-step restoring divider attached to EX. On a divide-class instruction, capture the post-forwarded operands and complete ID/EX metadata once, freeze younger fetch and decode, and let older MEM/WB work drain through invalid EX/MEM bubbles. When the divider finishes, advance exactly one EX/MEM result with the captured metadata; preserve dmem-stall priority and release the upstream interlock only when that result is accepted. Keep all RV32I operations and four MUL variants single-cycle. Implement exact RV32M signed, unsigned, divide-by-zero, and INT_MIN/-1 behavior. Preserve the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS result substitutions and exercise the same launch/complete interlock in formal mode. This should raise median Fmax with negligible change to CoreMark's marker-to-marker cycle count. \nRESULT: improvement 23.51 156.91 4491817\n\nhyp-20260923-002-r2s1 Move high-half multiply off the single-cycle EX path\nHYP: Keep MUL as a single-cycle 32-bit low-product operation. Move MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU into one shared iterative high-half multiplier attached to EX, removing their wide combinational products from the ALU. Form the unsigned 64-bit product with a 32-step shift-add engine using a roughly 33-bit adder; correct its high word for signed operands in separate completion cycles. Capture forwarded operands and RVFI metadata once, hold younger instructions while a high-half multiply runs, let older instructions drain, and present one completed EX/MEM result only when the boundary accepts it. Keep the result stable through dmem backpressure. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, use the exact prescribed stand-in result with a short but real start/complete handshake so the existing liveness depth remains meaningful. This should shorten the EX critical path while preserving low-half MUL throughput and ordered single-channel retirement. \nRESULT: regression -3.45 151.49 4491735\n\nhyp-20260923-002-r3s1 Deco\n…[3850 of 6922 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:fe13e449ece49f80]…\nse median Fmax enough to more than offset that cycle cost by removing the instruction-memory-to-register-file timing path. \nRESULT: regression -25.63 140.15 4805217\n\nhyp-20260923-002-r7s1 Replicate the register file for local read ports\nHYP: Replace the shared two-read register array with two independent, mirrored 31-by-32 flop arrays inside reg_file. Broadcast every accepted WB write and reset to both arrays; drive rs1 only from copy A and rs2 only from copy B. Preserve x0 as a literal zero and preserve each port's same-cycle write-first bypass. Keep the copies physically distinct through synthesis, checking the mapped netlist rather than relying on source-level duplication alone. This should let placement cluster each copy with its corresponding ID/EX operand register and remove cross-port fanout and routing from the current critical read path. Fetch, EX, MEM, forwarding, stalls, retirement timing, the nret=1 RVFI interface, and all RV32IM results remain unchanged. \nRESULT: regression -16.48 153.28 4491817\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
