9-23 hyp-20260923-003-r3s2 (regression, -3.64%): A 32-entry direction table saved roughly 6% of CoreMark cycles, but same-cycle B/J predecode, counter lookup, and PC-plus-immediate target selection in IF coincided\n…[2632 of 5704 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d14eb8692f01e4c4]…\ndelaying misprediction repair removes live IF checks, but the added IF PC selection and predicted-taken gating on the same-cycle EX redirect can still cost more timing than the hot-loop fetch cycles saved on CoreMark.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-001-r8s0 (regression, -8.17%): Retiming the register file's write-first bypass into EX adds a 32-bit WB capture and a conditional operand mux below forwarding, so shortening the ID read path can lose FPGA fitness when CoreMark gains no cycles.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-002-r8s1 (regression, -3.17%): Precomputing BRANCH/JAL targets in ID adds a PC-plus-immediate adder and opcode-gated mux before ID/EX; with CoreMark cycles unchanged, the lower Fmax outweighed the shorter EX redirect path.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-003-r8s2 (regression, -38.31%): Even replay after a resolved branch can lose fitness when EX-qualified cached-instruction and successor muxes feed IF/ID in the same cycle; roughly 5% fewer CoreMark cycles could not offset Fmax falling from 183.52 to 107.4 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
