{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"rg -n 'predictor|hyp-20260923-003-r9s2|hyp-20260923-003-r7s2|hyp-20260923-003-r6s2|hyp-20260923-003-r4s2|hyp-20260923-003-r3s2|hyp-20260923-003-r1s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260923-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict control flow with a small branch target buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current fetch PC advances by four until EX resolves every taken branch or jump. Each taken control transfer flushes the younger instruction, wasting a cycle in a branch-heavy CoreMark loop. A small predictor can remove that bubble on repeated control transfers without changing retirement or the memory interface.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current fetch PC advances by four until EX resolves every taken branch or jump. Each taken control transfer flushes the younger instruction, wasting a cycle in a branch-heavy CoreMark loop. A small predictor can remove that bubble on repeated control transfers without changing retirement or the memory interface.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 16-entry direct-mapped, fully tagged branch target buffer at fetch. Each entry holds a target and a two-bit saturating direction counter; unconditional JAL/JALR entries always predict taken. On a tag miss, fetch proceeds sequentially. Train only when a valid control-flow instruction advances through EX, using its resolved aligned target and outcome. Carry the exact predicted next PC with the instruction into EX, and redirect/flush younger work only when it differs from the resolved next PC. Suppress training and recovery while EX is held by a dmem stall, then perform them when EX advances. Preserve the existing sequential next PC and trap behavior for misaligned targets, and correct stale predictions even if the instruction at a trained PC changes. This should save cycles on recurring CoreMark branches and calls while keeping the added lookup small enough to limit Fmax loss.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the tagged 16-entry target/direction table, predict the fetch successor PC, and give EX recovery priority over fetch stalls. Reset entry valid bits and update entries only for accepted EX res\n…[52443 of 55515 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1e609ebcff15cb87]…\nv\", \"description\": \"Wire predictor lookup, accepted-EX training, and registered repair without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Check warm taken loops, alternating outcomes, aliasing, misaligned targets, repair under bus stalls, wrong-path suppression, and exact RVFI PCs and order.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 380.17, \"delta_pct\": -6.95, \"vs_baseline\": 34.42, \"fmax_mhz\": 174.92, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4601157, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5806, \"ff\": 2274, \"seeds\": [174.92, 167.59, 192.05], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-23T18:36:13.866511Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-003-r9s2 (regression, -6.95%): Predicting branch direction only in EX retains the taken-branch redirect bubble on correct guesses while mispredictions add a registered repair cycle, so a direction table needs enough PC-path timing gain to offset its cycle cost.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
