{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260923-003-r10s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260923-003-r10s2\ntitle: \"Predict same-page backward branches with a short target adder\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Repeated taken branches cost a fetch bubble in the current EX-resolved pipeline.\n  Earlier predictors saved roughly 4-6% of CoreMark cycles, but full-width\n  target selection, table lookup, or successor-PC comparison reduced Fmax more\n  than the cycle savings helped. Backward loop branches offer a narrower case:\n  their direction can be predicted without a table, and a same-page target\n  needs only a short addition in the fetch-to-PC path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Statically predict taken only for a valid, legal backward conditional BRANCH\n  whose target is aligned and remains within the current 4 KiB code page.\n  In IF, form the signed B-immediate plus PC[11:0] with a 13-bit adder; require\n  no page underflow and a word-aligned result. On an accepted fetch, select\n  the resulting low 12 target bits while retaining PC[31:12]. Other fetches\n  keep the existing PC+4 path. Add no history table, target RAM, or full-width\n  predicted-versus-resolved PC comparison.\n\n  Carry one predicted-taken bit with the instruction into EX. EX still resolves\n  the real branch condition using forwarded operands and reports its exact\n  architectural next PC in RVFI. Suppress the ordinary redirect for a correct\n  prediction; on an untaken prediction, redirect immediately to branch PC+4\n  and squash the fetched target instruction. Unpredicted taken branches use\n  the existing target redirect. Permit prediction only when IF/ID advances;\n  preserve the bit across stalls and give older EX recovery priority. Leave\n  misaligned targets and illegal branch encodings on their existing trap paths.\n  This should recover hot-loop fetch cycles with substantially less PC-path\n  logic than the prior predictor attempts, though Fmax remains the main risk.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Detect eligible backward BRANCH words, calculate only the 13-bit page-local target, and update the fetch PC on an accepted prediction with older recovery priority.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Carry a single predicted-taken bit in the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch the prediction bit with its accepted instruction and preserve it through holds while clearing it on bubbles.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compare actual branch direction with the latched prediction, suppress redirects on correct predictions, and choose target or fall-through for immediate recovery while preserving RVFI and trap semantics.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Squash only younger wrong-path work on a prediction miss and preserve existing bus, load-use, and divider stall behavior.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire prediction eligibility, stage acceptance, and EX recovery without changing the nret=1 ports.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Check warm taken loops, untaken exits, page-crossing and misaligned targets, reserved branch funct3 values, bus stalls, wrong-path side effects, and RVFI order and PCs.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
