{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=d['hypothesis']; h=h['hypothesis'] if isinstance(h,dict) else h\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'],d['title'],'\\\\n',h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20260923-003-r1s2 Predict control flow with a small branch target buffer \n Add a 16-entry direct-mapped, fully tagged branch target buffer at fetch. Each entry holds a target and a two-bit saturating direction counter; unconditional JAL/JALR entries always predict taken. On a tag miss, fetch proceeds sequentially. Train only when a valid control-flow instruction advances through EX, using its resolved aligned target and outcome. Carry the exact predicted next PC with the instruction into EX, and redirect/flush younger work only when it differs from the resolved next PC. Suppress training and recovery while EX is held by a dmem stall, then perform them when EX advances. Preserve the existing sequential next PC and trap behavior for misaligned targets, and correct stale predictions even if the instruction at a trained PC changes. This should save cycles on recurring CoreMark branches and calls while keeping the added lookup small enough to limit Fmax loss.\n\n\n hyp-20260923-003-r2s2 Add a tagged branch target buffer to the iterative-divider core \n Add the previously validated 16-entry direct-mapped, fully tagged branch\ntarget buffer to the current iterative-divider pipeline. Store a target\nand two-bit direction counter per entry; predict trained conditional\nbranches from the counter and JAL/JALR as taken. On a miss, fetch PC+4.\nCarry the predicted successor PC with each instruction and compare it\nagainst the resolved successor in EX. Redirect and flush younger work\nonly on a mismatch, including stale entries for non-control instructions.\nTrain or invalidate only when EX advances, so dmem stalls cannot duplicate\nupdates or lose recovery. Preserve sequential trap behavior for misaligned\ntargets. The divider's LUT reduction should let the measured BTB cycle\ngain improve fitness without exceeding the area limit.\n\n\n hyp-20260923-003-r3s2 Predict direct branches with a direction-only history table \n Replace the target-buffer approach with a 32-entry, untagged table of\ntwo-bit saturating direction counters, indexed by\n…[8976 of 12048 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:545dad1e19c96feb]…\nuire\nno page underflow and a word-aligned result. On an accepted fetch, select\nthe resulting low 12 target bits while retaining PC[31:12]. Other fetches\nkeep the existing PC+4 path. Add no history table, target RAM, or full-width\npredicted-versus-resolved PC comparison.\n\nCarry one predicted-taken bit with the instruction into EX. EX still resolves\nthe real branch condition using forwarded operands and reports its exact\narchitectural next PC in RVFI. Suppress the ordinary redirect for a correct\nprediction; on an untaken prediction, redirect immediately to branch PC+4\nand squash the fetched target instruction. Unpredicted taken branches use\nthe existing target redirect. Permit prediction only when IF/ID advances;\npreserve the bit across stalls and give older EX recovery priority. Leave\nmisaligned targets and illegal branch encodings on their existing trap paths.\nThis should recover hot-loop fetch cycles with substantially less PC-path\nlogic than the prior predictor attempts, though Fmax remains the main risk.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
