r3s2 Predict direct branches with a direction-only history table regression -3.64 146.61 4203461 5893\n   Replace the target-buffer approach with a 32-entry, untagged table of two-bit saturating direction counters, indexed by fetch PC[6:2]. Initialize counters weakly not taken. In IF, recognize only legal conditional BRANCH encodings and JAL; predict a BRANCH from its counter, predict JAL taken, and leave JALR sequential. Form a predicted taken target directly from the fetched B/J immediate and PC. Carry only one predicted-taken bit through ID/EX. In EX, compare that bit with the resolved control-flow outcome, treating a misaligned target trap as s\n  notes: \n…[4388 of 7460 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7ae5425cb1d5a47b]…\nwith a 13-bit adder; require no page underflow and a word-aligned result. On an accepted fetch, select the resulting low 12 target bits while retaining PC[31:12]. Other fetches keep the existing PC+4 path. Add no history table, target RAM, or full-width predicted-versus-resolved PC comparison.  Carry one predicted-taken bit with the instruction into \n  notes: \nhyp-20260923-003-r11s2 Predict short direct jumps at fetch regression -19.08 146.93 4444081 5688\n   Predict only an accepted JAL whose signed displacement fits 12 bits, whose target stays in the current 4 KiB page, and whose target is word aligned. In IF, recognize the JAL opcode, check the displacement sign extension, and use a 13-bit addition of PC[11:0] and the displacement to prove that no page boundary is crossed. Select the page-local target for the next fetch; keep the existing sequential path for every other instruction, including JALR. Carry one predicted-JAL bit with the instruction. In EX, retain the exact target, link value, and R\n  notes: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
