{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -c \"git status --short -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260923-003-r13s2.yaml; sed -n '1,110p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260923-003-r13s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260923-003-r13s2\ntitle: \"Cache block-local loop targets without fetch instruction decode\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current core spends 4,491,817 cycles on CoreMark because each taken\n  branch redirects fetch from EX. Predicting short backward branches reduced\n  that count to about 4.30 million, but decoding the live imem word and adding\n  its offset on the fetch PC path lowered Fmax to about 172 MHz. A small cache\n  of already resolved loop targets could retain much of the cycle saving while\n  keeping imem data and target arithmetic out of the PC update path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add an eight-entry direct-mapped target cache for legal, aligned, backward\n  conditional branches whose resolved target is in the same 256-byte block\n  as the branch. Index with PC[4:2]. Each entry holds a valid bit, PC[15:5]\n  tag, target PC[7:0], the twelve B-immediate encoding bits, and a one-bit\n  last-outcome direction. Train only when a valid EX instruction advances;\n  update direction on both taken and untaken outcomes. On a valid tag hit\n  with taken direction, fetch uses {PC[31:8], cached_target[7:0]} as its\n  successor, without inspecting io_imemData or adding an immediate. A hit\n  carries only predicted-taken and cached immediate bits through ID/EX.\n\n  EX checks that the predicted instruction is still a legal, aligned,\n  taken branch with the same immediate bits. Because training requires a\n  target in the same block and the lookup matches the branch PC's low byte,\n  that check proves the cached low-byte target correct even if high PC bits\n  alias in the partial tag. On a mismatch, use the existing redirect to the\n  resolved target or PC+4 and squash younger work; unpredicted branches use\n  the existing redirect. Give older EX recovery priority, retain prediction\n  metadata across stalls, and never train on invalid or held EX work. Keep\n  RVFI next PC, traps, and retirement order derived from resolved execution.\n  The goal is to retain several percent of branch-cycle savings while\n  recovering enough Fmax to exceed the current 408.57 fitness.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 2\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Implement the eight-entry PC-indexed loop target cache, block-local prediction, and accepted-EX training; preserve redirect priority and fetch stalls.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Carry predicted-taken and cached B-immediate signature through the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch prediction metadata with the fetched instruction and clear it on invalid or flushed entries while preserving it on holds.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Verify predicted branches against resolved condition, legality, alignment, and immediate signature; suppress only correct redirects and repair all mismatches.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Connect EX training and prediction verification to fetch and hazard control without changing the single-retire interface.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Flush younger work on misprediction and qualify predictor updates with EX advancement through bus and divide holds.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Check repeated taken loops, untaken exits, table collisions, changed instructions, block boundaries, stalls, wrong-path effects, and RVFI PC and order.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
