{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nids={'hyp-20260923-003-r2s2','hyp-20260923-003-r3s2','hyp-20260923-003-r7s2','hyp-20260923-003-r9s2','hyp-20260923-003-r10s2'}\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n x=json.loads(l)\n if x.get('id') in ids:\n  print('\\\\nID',x['id'],'TITLE',x['title'])\n  print('MOTIVATION',x['motivation'])\n  print('HYPOTHESIS',x['hypothesis']['hypothesis'] if isinstance(x['hypothesis'],dict) else x['hypothesis'])\n  print('NOTES',x.get('implementation_notes'))\n  print('METRICS', {k:x.get(k) for k in ['fitness','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','seeds']})\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID hyp-20260923-003-r2s2 TITLE Add a tagged branch target buffer to the iterative-divider core\nMOTIVATION The earlier 16-entry BTB reduced CoreMark cycles from 4491485 to 4234052,\nbut its pre-divider implementation used 10412 LUT4s and scored 348.08.\nThe current iterative-divider core scores 349.32 with only 5575 LUT4s,\nleaving room to recover the measured control-flow cycle savings within\nthe 10000-LUT4 limit.\n\nHYPOTHESIS Add the previously validated 16-entry direct-mapped, fully tagged branch\ntarget buffer to the current iterative-divider pipeline. Store a target\nand two-bit direction counter per entry; predict trained conditional\nbranches from the counter and JAL/JALR as taken. On a miss, fetch PC+4.\nCarry the predicted successor PC with each instruction and compare it\nagainst the resolved successor in EX. Redirect and flush younger work\nonly on a mismatch, including stale entries for non-control instructions.\nTrain or invalidate only when EX advances, so dmem stalls cannot duplicate\nupdates or lose recovery. Preserve sequential trap behavior for misaligned\ntargets. The divider's LUT reduction should let the measured BTB cycle\ngain improve fitness without exceeding the area limit.\n\nNOTES \nMETRICS {'fitness': 336.82, 'delta_pct': -3.58, 'fmax_mhz': 142.61, 'cycles': 4234021, 'lut4': 6042, 'ff': 2385, 'seeds': [143.0, 141.88, 142.61]}\n\nID hyp-20260923-003-r3s2 TITLE Predict direct branches with a direction-only history table\nMOTIVATION The previous 16-entry target buffer reduced CoreMark from 4,491,817 to\n4,234,021 timed cycles, but on the iterative-divider core it lowered Fmax\nfrom 156.91 to 142.61 MHz and reduced fitness to 336.82. Direct branch and\nJAL targets are encoded in the fetched instruction, so storing 32-bit\ntargets and comparing tags adds hardware that these transfers do not need.\n\nHYPOTHESIS Replace the target-buffer approach with a 32-entry, untagged table of\ntwo-bit saturating direction counters, indexed by fetch PC[6:2]. Initialize\ncounters weakly not taken. In IF, recognize only legal conditio\n…[5627 of 8699 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ae585821cf7556e7]…\nep the existing PC+4 path. Add no history table, target RAM, or full-width\npredicted-versus-resolved PC comparison.\n\nCarry one predicted-taken bit with the instruction into EX. EX still resolves\nthe real branch condition using forwarded operands and reports its exact\narchitectural next PC in RVFI. Suppress the ordinary redirect for a correct\nprediction; on an untaken prediction, redirect immediately to branch PC+4\nand squash the fetched target instruction. Unpredicted taken branches use\nthe existing target redirect. Permit prediction only when IF/ID advances;\npreserve the bit across stalls and give older EX recovery priority. Leave\nmisaligned targets and illegal branch encodings on their existing trap paths.\nThis should recover hot-loop fetch cycles with substantially less PC-path\nlogic than the prior predictor attempts, though Fmax remains the main risk.\n\nNOTES \nMETRICS {'fitness': 401.26, 'delta_pct': -1.79, 'fmax_mhz': 172.92, 'cycles': 4309463, 'lut4': 5738, 'ff': 2209, 'seeds': [172.92, 166.67, 174.61]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
