{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nids={'hyp-20260923-003-r4s2','hyp-20260923-003-r5s2','hyp-20260923-003-r6s2','hyp-20260923-003-r8s2','hyp-20260923-003-r11s2'}\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n x=json.loads(l)\n if x.get('id') in ids:\n  print('\\\\n',x['id'],x['title']);print(x['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20260923-003-r4s2 Predict hot backward loops with one target register\nAdd one fully tagged loop-target entry containing a valid bit, the branch PC,\nand its resolved 32-bit target. Train it only when a valid, aligned, taken\nbackward conditional branch advances from EX; do not let jumps or forward\nbranches replace the entry. In IF, predict the stored target on an exact PC\nmatch and otherwise use PC+4. This removes fetched-instruction predecode,\ndirection-table lookup, and an IF target adder from the earlier predictor's\ntiming path. Carry the exact predicted successor PC into EX and redirect only\nwhen it differs from the resolved successor, including loop exit, stale code,\nand misaligned-target cases. Invalidate a stale entry when its matching\ninstruction is no longer the same aligned backward branch; update or recover\nonly when EX advances, with redirect taking priority over fetch stalls. The\nsingle entry should capture the hottest loop while limiting area and Fmax\ncost enough for the cycle saving to improve CoreMark iterations per second.\n\n\n hyp-20260923-003-r5s2 Look ahead one fetch for branch target prediction\nAdd a 16-entry, fully tagged branch target buffer with two-bit direction\ncounters. During each ordinary fetch, look up PC+4 and register the hit,\ndirection, and target for the following sequential fetch. The current\nfetch selects between PC+4 and this registered target, so table selection\nand tag comparison are outside its same-cycle PC update path. Hold the\nlookahead record when fetch stalls, and invalidate it after a predicted\ntarget or an EX recovery; those nonsequential fetches proceed without a\nprediction until sequential lookahead is ready again. Train once when a\nvalid branch or jump advances from EX, and invalidate an entry when its\nmatching PC executes a non-control instruction.\n\nCarry the selected successor PC with the instruction. In EX, compare it\nagainst the resolved successor, including misaligned-target traps and\nchanging JALR targets, but register the recovery flag and target instead\n\n…[3208 of 6280 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0e944006af4320ef]…\nnly an accepted JAL whose signed displacement fits 12 bits, whose\ntarget stays in the current 4 KiB page, and whose target is word aligned.\nIn IF, recognize the JAL opcode, check the displacement sign extension, and\nuse a 13-bit addition of PC[11:0] and the displacement to prove that no page\nboundary is crossed. Select the page-local target for the next fetch; keep\nthe existing sequential path for every other instruction, including JALR.\nCarry one predicted-JAL bit with the instruction. In EX, retain the exact\ntarget, link value, and RVFI next PC, but suppress the ordinary redirect\nonly for that bit. The prediction uses the current instruction word, so it\ncannot become stale when code changes and needs no target comparison or\nmisprediction recovery. Apply prediction only when fetch advances; older EX\nredirects and all bus, divide, and load-use holds retain their priority.\nThis should remove a taken-jump bubble for common nearby jumps while\nkeeping the added PC path narrower than the prior branch predictors.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
