cessor in EX. Redirect and flush younger work only on a mismatch, including stale entries for non-control instructions. Train or invalidate only when EX advances, so dmem stalls cannot duplicate updates or lose recovery. Preserve sequential trap behavior for misaligned targets. The divider's LUT reduction should let the measured BTB cycle gain improve fitness without exceeding the area limit. \nhyp-20260923-003-r3s2 regression 348.78 146.61 4203461 Predict direct branches with a direction-only history table\n  Replace the target-buffer approach with a 32-entry, untagged table of \n…[10338 of 13410 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0ec58e34a2efabc6]…\ns a target in the same block and the lookup matches the branc\nhyp-20260923-003-r14s2 improvement 435.24 188.61 4333465 Predict backward branches with low-byte PC steering\n  Statically predict taken for a fetched BRANCH opcode with a negative offset and a word-aligned speculative target, except when PC[7:0] is 8'hfc. Add the low eight bits of the B immediate to PC[7:0] and select only those eight bits for the next fetch PC; let PC[31:8] follow the existing PC+4 path. Excluding the last word of a 256-byte block makes the sequential high bits equal the current high bits, so prediction adds no wide high-PC mux. Do not put immediate-size or block-underflow checks on the fetch-PC selection path. Capture the adder carry and a signed-eight-bit-offset check as prediction metadata alongside the instruction.  In EX, a prediction is correct only if the instruction is a valid, legal, aligned, taken branch, the offset fits signed eight bits, and the captured low-byte addition carried out of the block. Otherwise redirect t\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
