{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"rg -n -i 'predictor|prediction|btb|branch.*target|backward' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260923-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict control flow with a small branch target buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current fetch PC advances by four until EX resolves every taken branch or jump. Each taken control transfer flushes the younger instruction, wasting a cycle in a branch-heavy CoreMark loop. A small predictor can remove that bubble on repeated control transfers without changing retirement or the memory interface.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current fetch PC advances by four until EX resolves every taken branch or jump. Each taken control transfer flushes the younger instruction, wasting a cycle in a branch-heavy CoreMark loop. A small predictor can remove that bubble on repeated control transfers without changing retirement or the memory interface.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 16-entry direct-mapped, fully tagged branch target buffer at fetch. Each entry holds a target and a two-bit saturating direction counter; unconditional JAL/JALR entries always predict taken. On a tag miss, fetch proceeds sequentially. Train only when a valid control-flow instruction advances through EX, using its resolved aligned target and outcome. Carry the exact predicted next PC with the instruction into EX, and redirect/flush younger work only when it differs from the resolved next PC. Suppress training and recovery while EX is held by a dmem stall, then perform them when EX advances. Preserve the existing sequential next PC and trap behavior for misaligned targets, and correct stale predictions even if the instruction at a trained PC changes. This should save cycles on recurring CoreMark branches and calls while keeping the added lookup small enough to limit Fmax loss.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the tagged 16-entry target/direction table, predict the fetch successor PC, and give EX recovery priority over fetch stalls. Reset entry valid bits and update entries only for accepted EX res\n…[30993 of 34065 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a07e409a664007bd]…\nre.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire prediction, training, and accepted-EX control while preserving the nret=1 RVFI and memory interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Check warm loops and exits, stale instruction immediates, illegal branches, target alignment, bus stalls, wrong-path side effects, and RVFI PC and order.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 216.46, \"delta_pct\": -44.8, \"vs_baseline\": -23.46, \"fmax_mhz\": 93.34, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4312127, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5857, \"ff\": 2315, \"seeds\": [91.35, 94.51, 93.34], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-23T17:36:10.709848Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-003-r6s2 (regression, -44.80%): Registering a backward-branch lookahead still leaves live instruction opcode and B-immediate checks ahead of the IF PC target mux, so prediction can lose FPGA fitness despite moving the predecessor lookup out of the fetch cycle.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
