9-23 hyp-20260923-003-r3s2 (regression, -3.64%): A 32-entry direction table saved roughly 6% of CoreMark cycles, but same-cycle B/J predecode, counter lookup, and PC-plus-immediate target selection in IF coincided\n…[1826 of 4898 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6ef8764f7cdaea32]…\nds a refill bubble after imem backpressure and another taken-branch recovery bubble, so those CoreMark cycle costs can outweigh the timing benefit.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-003-r6s2 (regression, -44.80%): Registering a backward-branch lookahead still leaves live instruction opcode and B-immediate checks ahead of the IF PC target mux, so prediction can lose FPGA fitness despite moving the predecessor lookup out of the fetch cycle.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-002-r7s1 (regression, -16.48%): Mirroring the flop register file for separate rs1 and rs2 reads leaves the indexed read muxes intact while doubling write/reset loads and adding an rs2 inversion, so read-port locality alone can reduce FPGA fitness.\n- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-003-r7s2 (regression, -9.43%): Registering a one-entry loop target and delaying misprediction repair removes live IF checks, but the added IF PC selection and predicted-taken gating on the same-cycle EX redirect can still cost more timing than the hot-loop fetch cycles saved on CoreMark.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"rg -n 'predictor|BTB|branch|lookahead|r7s2|r6s2|r4s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260923-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Forward live MEM load data into EX\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The in-order pipeline inserts a bubble whenever an instruction immediately\\nconsumes a load result, even though the zero-wait memory model presents that\\nresult combinationally while the load is in MEM. CoreMark's list and matrix\\ncode repeatedly loads a value before using it. Removing these bubbles should\\nimprove iterations per cycle without changing the retirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The in-order pipeline inserts a bubble whenever an instruction immediately\\nconsumes a load result, even though the zero-wait memory model presents that\\nresult combinationally while the load is in MEM. CoreMark's list and matrix\\ncode repeatedly loads a value before using it. Removing these bubbles should\\nimprove iterations per cycle without changing the retirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a MEM-to-EX bypass for the current load's fully extracted and extended\\ndata, and let the dependent instruction enter EX directly behind the load.\\nWhen dmem is not ready, hold both instructions until the response is valid;\\nsuppress EX redirects during that hold. Do not forward a misaligned load\\nthat traps. Keep the existing EX/MEM priority for ordinary ALU results and\\nthe MEM/WB fallback for older writers. The bypass should reduce load-use\\nstalls while preserving RVFI operand values and exact memory semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the current load's sign- or zero-extended value and its alignment validity as combinational bypass outputs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Route the valid live load value from MEM to the existing EX/MEM forwarding input, while retaining ALU forwarding for other instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Prevent a misaligned trapping load from claiming EX/MEM forwarding priority over an older valid writer.\"}, {\"file\": \"rt\n…[52785 of 55857 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:88990a99e39c10e6]…\nr, with correct priority over imem and dmem stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Exercise warm loop predictions and exits, unpredicted branches and jumps, changed instructions or immediates, misaligned targets, both bus stalls, wrong-path side effects, and RVFI PC/order continuity.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 370.03, \"delta_pct\": -9.43, \"vs_baseline\": 30.84, \"fmax_mhz\": 160.08, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4326193, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5824, \"ff\": 2392, \"seeds\": [181.65, 159.52, 160.08], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-23T17:58:00.105263Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-23 hyp-20260923-003-r7s2 (regression, -9.43%): Registering a one-entry loop target and delaying misprediction repair removes live IF checks, but the added IF PC selection and predicted-taken gating on the same-cycle EX redirect can still cost more timing than the hot-loop fetch cycles saved on CoreMark.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
