{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'\nfor line in open(p):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{}); h=h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'],d['title'],'fitness',d.get('fitness'),'delta',d.get('delta_pct'),'fmax',d.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'),'lut',d.get('lut4'))\n  print(h[:900].replace('\\\\n',' '))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20260923-003-r1s2 Predict control flow with a small branch target buffer fitness 348.08 delta 23.07 fmax 147.38 cycles 4234052 lut 10412\nAdd a 16-entry direct-mapped, fully tagged branch target buffer at fetch. Each entry holds a target and a two-bit saturating direction counter; unconditional JAL/JALR entries always predict taken. On a tag miss, fetch proceeds sequentially. Train only when a valid control-flow instruction advances through EX, using its resolved aligned target and outcome. Carry the exact predicted next PC with the instruction into EX, and redirect/flush younger work only when it differs from the resolved next PC. Suppress training and recovery while EX is held by a dmem stall, then perform them when EX advances. Preserve the existing sequential next PC and trap behavior for misaligned targets, and correct stale predictions even if the instruction at a trained PC changes. This should save cycles on recurring CoreMark branches and calls while keeping the added lookup small enough to limit Fmax loss. \n\n hyp-20260923-003-r2s2 Add a tagged branch target buffer to the iterative-divider core fitness 336.82 delta -3.58 fmax 142.61 cycles 4234021 lut 6042\nAdd the previously validated 16-entry direct-mapped, fully tagged branch target buffer to the current iterative-divider pipeline. Store a target and two-bit direction counter per entry; predict trained conditional branches from the counter and JAL/JALR as taken. On a miss, fetch PC+4. Carry the predicted successor PC with each instruction and compare it against the resolved successor in EX. Redirect and flush younger work only on a mismatch, including stale entries for non-control instructions. Train or invalidate only when EX advances, so dmem stalls cannot duplicate updates or lose recovery. Preserve sequential trap behavior for misaligned targets. The divider's LUT reduction should let the measured BTB cycle gain improve fitness without exceeding the area limit. \n\n hyp-20260923-003-r3s2 Predict direct branches with a direction-only history tabl\n…[4119 of 7191 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:127b1d210c1865ef]…\nct one hot loop with registered target and selective repair fitness 370.03 delta -9.43 fmax 160.08 cycles 4326193 lut 5824\nCache one aligned, taken backward conditional branch: its exact branch PC, resolved target, and the 12 raw B-immediate bits that determine that target. When an accepted sequential fetch is at branch_pc-4, register a lookahead hit and target. On the following fetch at branch_pc, select the registered target using only the registered hit; do not decode or inspect the live imem word in the PC selection path. Hold the lookahead through imem/decode stalls and discard it on any nonsequential PC change. Carry a predicted-taken bit and the cached immediate signature with that instruction into EX.  Keep the existing same-cycle EX redirect for all unpredicted branches and jumps, so misses retain today's penalty. For a predicted instruction, check in EX that it is a valid, aligned, taken conditional branch with the same B-immediate signature. A matching PC and immediate prove that its computed targ\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
