{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260923-003-r9s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260923-003-r9s2\ntitle: \"Predict branch direction at execute to shorten PC steering\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Earlier fetch-side predictors saved roughly four to six percent of CoreMark\n  cycles but lost more fitness to added fetch-PC and recovery timing. The\n  current EX branch comparison also feeds the same-cycle redirect path.\n  Predicting only the branch decision at the existing EX redirect point\n  avoids a target table, live fetch decode, and another normal fetch-PC mux.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a 16-entry, two-bit saturating direction table indexed by branch PC.\n  Read it in ID and latch one predicted-taken bit with the branch. In EX,\n  use that registered bit to steer the existing redirect to the target for\n  an aligned conditional branch; retain the ordinary redirect for JAL and\n  JALR. Compute the true comparison in parallel for architectural RVFI\n  state and train the table only when a valid branch advances. Register a\n  correction PC when prediction differs from the true outcome, then give\n  that correction priority on the following cycle and squash every younger\n  wrong-path instruction before it can retire, write memory, or start DIV.\n  A predicted taken branch with a misaligned target must follow the trap\n  path instead of fetching that target. Preserve the exact resolved PC and\n  trap in RVFI, plus redirect priority across both bus stalls. Correct\n  predictions keep today's branch cycle count; the proposed gain is a\n  shorter EX-to-PC timing path, while rare mispredictions cost a repair\n  cycle. This isolates the predictor from the fetch path that dominated\n  previous regressions.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 2\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n    description: \"Add a small direction-counter table with ID lookup and one accepted-branch update per EX instruction.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Carry the predicted-taken bit with the ID/EX instruction.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch the direction prediction with its valid branch, preserving it through holds and bubbles.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use predicted direction for normal conditional-branch PC steering, compare actual direction in parallel, and register the exact repair PC on a mismatch.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Apply registered repair before the ordinary redirect and fetch stall, without adding predictor lookup to the normal fetch path.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Squash younger work during a repair cycle and prevent wrong-path EX side effects while preserving stall priority.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire predictor lookup, accepted-EX training, and registered repair without changing the nret=1 interface.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Check warm taken loops, alternating outcomes, aliasing, misaligned targets, repair under bus stalls, wrong-path suppression, and exact RVFI PCs and order.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
